WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.705
Picture the restaurant's manager.

2
00:00:04.705 --> 00:00:09.990
She wants to know where the evening goes, without asking anyone to fill in a timesheet.

3
00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.299
Nobody fills those in honestly anyway.

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:19.964
So every few seconds, she glances through the kitchen door, and writes down what each cook is doing, and for which dish.

5
00:00:19.964 --> 00:00:21.644
Ana, soup, chopping onions.

6
00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:24.825
After a night of glances, she counts.

7
00:00:24.825 --> 00:00:30.562
If a third of her notes say chopping onions, then about a third of the evening went on onions.

8
00:00:30.562 --> 00:00:33.125
No timesheets, and a pretty honest answer.

9
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.478
A sampling profiler does exactly that to your program.

10
00:00:37.478 --> 00:00:48.193
Every few milliseconds, it looks at each thread, a line of work inside the program, and writes down its call stack: the chain of methods the thread is inside right now.

11
00:00:48.193 --> 00:00:49.660
One glance is a sample.

12
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:57.441
And Ana, soup, chopping onions is a stack, read from the bottom up: the thread, the method, and the method that it called.

13
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:02.636
The profiler draws its answer as a flame graph: towers of boxes.

14
00:01:02.636 --> 00:01:06.106
And here's a question many of us are too shy to ask.

15
00:01:06.106 --> 00:01:07.240
Is it a timeline?

16
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.508
Did the box on the left run first?

17
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.727
Let's build one from scratch, so we can watch every step.

18
00:01:16.727 --> 00:01:20.502
The code is the report step from this lesson, readingSeconds.

19
00:01:20.502 --> 00:01:25.452
It checks a document's language with a regex, a small pattern language for text.

20
00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:27.308
It splits the text into words.

21
00:01:27.308 --> 00:01:30.897
And it formats a line for a debug log that's switched off.

22
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.874
Here's one thread's stack at the first glance.

23
00:01:34.874 --> 00:01:42.025
Thread.run at the bottom, then readingSeconds, then String.split, then indexOf, looking for the next space.

24
00:01:42.025 --> 00:01:44.364
The top frame is where the CPU was.

25
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:48.216
Every 10 milliseconds, another glance, another snapshot.

26
00:01:48.216 --> 00:01:49.520
Nine glances in all.

27
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.779
These nine are made up, to keep the picture small.

28
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.575
Then the profiler counts identical stacks.

29
00:01:55.575 --> 00:01:57.721
One line per stack, then its count.

30
00:01:57.721 --> 00:02:02.257
This text is called collapsed stacks, and every flame graph tool reads it.

31
00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:04.441
Now, our first glance caught split.

32
00:02:04.441 --> 00:02:08.560
When we draw the graph, will split be on the left, because it came first?

33
00:02:12.733 --> 00:02:17.075
The tool sorts the stacks alphabetically, by their full names.

34
00:02:17.075 --> 00:02:17.916
Not by time.

35
00:02:17.916 --> 00:02:24.778
java.lang.String.format comes before java.lang.String.split, and both come before java.util.regex.

36
00:02:24.778 --> 00:02:29.891
So our first glance, the split from 10 milliseconds, lands in the middle.

37
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:34.376
A flame graph is filed like a phone book, not written like a diary.

38
00:02:34.376 --> 00:02:36.738
Left to right tells you the alphabet.

39
00:02:36.738 --> 00:02:38.269
It never tells you when.

40
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:44.037
Then identical frames at the same depth, under the same parent, merge into one box.

41
00:02:44.037 --> 00:02:47.239
When each glance was taken is now gone for good.

42
00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.783
So, rule one: width is samples.

43
00:02:49.783 --> 00:02:53.445
format was on 3 of the 9 glances, so it's 3 wide.

44
00:02:53.445 --> 00:02:57.705
readingSeconds was on all 9, so it spans the whole graph.

45
00:02:57.933 --> 00:03:00.779
Rule two: left to right is the alphabet.

46
00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:09.104
The header of flamegraph.pl, the original drawing script, says it plainly: the ordering on the x-axis has no meaning.

47
00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:12.072
And that's where the manager's picture breaks.

48
00:03:12.072 --> 00:03:15.287
Her towers for the evening would line up by the clock.

49
00:03:15.287 --> 00:03:16.180
These never do.

50
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.345
Rule three: look at the tops.

51
00:03:18.345 --> 00:03:24.715
The part of a box with nothing on top of it is self time: the CPU was in that frame's own code.

52
00:03:24.715 --> 00:03:26.660
Wide tops are where to start.

53
00:03:26.867 --> 00:03:30.363
readingSeconds is wide, but it's covered by its children.

54
00:03:30.363 --> 00:03:33.430
It does little itself, and calls expensive things.

55
00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:38.215
So the fix is usually in one of its children, or in the decision to call them.

56
00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:49.409
Now a real profile of the same code: async-profiler, four threads, 10 seconds, 3,569 samples.

57
00:03:49.409 --> 00:03:54.933
readingSeconds covers 2,546 of them: 71.3 percent.

58
00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.532
Walk up from that wide box.

59
00:03:57.532 --> 00:04:08.834
Its widest children are String.split, with 24.2 percent of all samples, String.format, with 22.3, and Pattern.compile, with 13.6.

60
00:04:08.834 --> 00:04:14.003
And the bare end on the right is its own time: 257 samples.

61
00:04:14.233 --> 00:04:17.281
Notice where format sits: on the far left.

62
00:04:17.281 --> 00:04:19.095
Not because it ran first.

63
00:04:19.095 --> 00:04:20.909
Because f comes before s.

64
00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:23.900
One small tower beside it is worth a smile.

65
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:31.429
The demo's loop reads the clock before every call, to check its deadline, and 64 samples landed in reading the clock.

66
00:04:31.429 --> 00:04:33.681
The stopwatch shows up in the race.

67
00:04:33.681 --> 00:04:37.928
So when a box surprises you, first ask if it's your own measuring.

68
00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:39.415
Quick prediction.

69
00:04:39.415 --> 00:04:43.336
Pattern.compile builds the same regex on every call.

70
00:04:43.336 --> 00:04:46.805
Move it into a static final field, built once.

71
00:04:46.805 --> 00:04:48.464
Which boxes disappear?

72
00:04:51.433 --> 00:04:55.709
The Pattern.compile tower goes: 13.6 percent of the samples.

73
00:04:55.709 --> 00:05:01.196
But Pattern.matcher stays, because a new matcher is still made on every call.

74
00:05:01.196 --> 00:05:04.403
The next lesson runs exactly that experiment.

75
00:05:05.333 --> 00:05:06.485
A few guardrails.

76
00:05:06.485 --> 00:05:09.872
A flame graph shows only the seconds you recorded.

77
00:05:09.872 --> 00:05:14.749
Width counts everything above a box, so a wide box isn't slow by itself.

78
00:05:14.749 --> 00:05:16.645
Left to right is never time.

79
00:05:16.645 --> 00:05:19.896
And a CPU profile barely sees threads that wait.

80
00:05:19.896 --> 00:05:23.080
For those, you take a different kind of sample.

81
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.245
So, is a flame graph a timeline?

82
00:05:26.245 --> 00:05:26.456
No.

83
00:05:26.456 --> 00:05:29.402
It's the manager's tally, drawn as towers.

84
00:05:29.402 --> 00:05:32.840
Width is time, and left to right is the alphabet.

85
00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:36.769
Start at the widest top, and walk down to your own code.

86
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:37.869
Quick check.

87
00:05:37.869 --> 00:05:42.501
A narrow box sits at the far left of a flame graph's second row.

88
00:05:42.501 --> 00:05:43.732
Did it run first?

89
00:05:43.732 --> 00:05:44.963
Did it cost much?

90
00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:48.705
Neither.

91
00:05:48.705 --> 00:05:51.279
Far left means its name sorts early.

92
00:05:51.279 --> 00:05:54.425
Narrow means few samples, so it cost little.
